---
import ArticlePage from '../../components/ArticlePage.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../utils/paths';
import { USGA_EXPECTED_SCORE_URL, USGA_HANDICAP_INDEX_URL } from '../../data/handicapSources';
import {
  ENGLAND_GOLF_INDEX_URL,
  ENGLAND_GOLF_PCC_URL,
  GOLF_DIGEST_2024_URL,
  HANDICAP_GUIDE_UPDATED,
  RANDA_RULE_5_URL,
  USGA_2024_CHANGES_URL,
  USGA_COURSE_HANDICAP_URL,
  USGA_DIFFERENTIAL_EXAMPLE_URL,
  USGA_NET_DOUBLE_BOGEY_URL,
  USGA_PCC_URL,
  WA_GOLF_DIFFERENTIAL_URL,
} from '../../data/whsSources';
import { HANDICAP_CALLS, HANDICAP_TARGET } from '../../data/storeCalls';

const FEW_ROUNDS: readonly (readonly [string, string])[] = [
  ['3', 'Lowest 1, minus 2.0'],
  ['4', 'Lowest 1, minus 1.0'],
  ['5', 'Lowest 1'],
  ['6', 'Average of lowest 2, minus 1.0'],
  ['7 to 8', 'Average of lowest 2'],
  ['9 to 11', 'Average of lowest 3'],
  ['12 to 14', 'Average of lowest 4'],
  ['15 to 16', 'Average of lowest 5'],
  ['17 to 18', 'Average of lowest 6'],
  ['19', 'Average of lowest 7'],
  ['20', 'Average of lowest 8'],
];

const SAMPLE_DIFFERENTIALS: readonly (readonly [string, string, string, string])[] = [
  ['1', '95', '71.5 / 125', '21.2'],
  ['2', '92', '70.8 / 121', '19.8'],
  ['3', '98', '72.4 / 131', '22.1'],
  ['4', '90', '71.5 / 125', '16.7'],
  ['5', '94', '69.9 / 118', '23.1'],
];
---
<ArticlePage
  pageTitle="How Is a Golf Handicap Calculated? Formula & Examples"
  description="Understand score differentials, the best 8 of 20, fewer-round adjustments, caps and nine-hole scores under the World Handicap System, with worked examples and sources."
  eyebrow="Guide · Golf handicap"
  heading="How a golf handicap is calculated"
  published={HANDICAP_GUIDE_UPDATED}
  banner={HANDICAP_TARGET}
  calls={HANDICAP_CALLS}
>
  <p>
    A golf handicap tells you how many strokes above a course's difficulty you play on a good day. Under the World Handicap System
    (WHS), used by the USGA, the R&amp;A and national associations around the world, every round you post becomes one number called a
    score differential, and your Handicap Index is built from the best of your recent differentials. This guide walks through each
    step with real numbers and links every rule to the body that publishes it.
  </p>

  <h2>Handicap Index, Course Handicap and Playing Handicap</h2>
  <p>Three numbers get called "your handicap", and they do different jobs:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Handicap Index</strong> is your portable number, the same at every course, for example 15.0. It is what this guide calculates.</li>
    <li><strong>Course Handicap</strong> is how many strokes you get from a particular set of tees. It changes with the course's difficulty.</li>
    <li><strong>Playing Handicap</strong> is your Course Handicap after any allowance the competition sets, for example in a fourball.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 1: Calculate an 18-hole score differential</h2>
  <p>Each 18-hole round becomes a score differential with this formula (<a href={WA_GOLF_DIFFERENTIAL_URL}>WA Golf: score differential</a>):</p>
  <p><strong>Score differential = (113 ÷ Slope Rating) × (Adjusted Gross Score − Course Rating − PCC adjustment)</strong></p>
  <p>The result is rounded to one decimal place.</p>

  <h3>Adjusted gross score</h3>
  <p>
    Your adjusted gross score is your total after capping any hole that went badly. For handicap purposes the most you can count on a
    hole is <strong>net double bogey</strong>: par + 2 + any handicap strokes you receive on that hole. A player who doesn't have a
    Handicap Index yet caps each hole at <strong>par + 5</strong>
    (<a href={USGA_NET_DOUBLE_BOGEY_URL}>USGA Rules of Handicapping, Rule 3.1</a>).
  </p>
  <p>
    Example: on a par 4 where you receive one stroke, net double bogey is 4 + 2 + 1 = 7. If you made a 9 there, you count 7, and
    your adjusted gross score is two strokes lower than your actual total.
  </p>

  <h3>Course Rating, Slope Rating and PCC</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Course Rating</strong> is what a scratch golfer is expected to shoot from your tees, for example 71.5. It is on the scorecard or the club's website.</li>
    <li><strong>Slope Rating</strong> says how much harder the course plays for a bogey golfer than for a scratch golfer. 113 is standard; most courses sit between about 100 and 140. A higher slope makes the same score count for less.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>PCC</strong>, the playing conditions calculation, adjusts for days when the course played unusually hard or easy, such
      as high wind. It runs from −1.0 to +3.0, only scores from players with an index of 36.0 or below count towards it, it needs at
      least 8 scores that day, and on most days it is 0.0 (<a href={USGA_PCC_URL}>USGA: what is the PCC</a>). England Golf notes that
      the PCC is under review (<a href={ENGLAND_GOLF_PCC_URL}>England Golf: PCC</a>).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Worked example: from scorecard to differential</h2>
  <p>
    You shoot an adjusted gross score of 95 from tees with a Course Rating of 71.5 and a Slope Rating of 125, on a day with a PCC
    of 0.0. The USGA uses this exact example (<a href={USGA_DIFFERENTIAL_EXAMPLE_URL}>USGA Rules of Handicapping</a>):
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>95 − 71.5 − 0.0 = 23.5 strokes above the Course Rating.</li>
    <li>113 ÷ 125 = 0.904, because the course is harder than standard.</li>
    <li>0.904 × 23.5 = 21.244, rounded to <strong>21.2</strong>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The same 95 on a course with a slope of 113 and a rating of 71.5 would give 23.5. The harder the course, the less a score above
    its rating counts against you. That is why the WHS uses your lowest <em>differentials</em>, not your lowest raw scores: a 92 on
    a tough course can beat an 88 on an easy one.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 2: Select differentials from your scoring record</h2>
  <p>
    With 20 or more scores, your Handicap Index is the average of the best 8 differentials from your most recent 20
    (<a href={ENGLAND_GOLF_INDEX_URL}>England Golf: your WHS index</a>, <a href={USGA_HANDICAP_INDEX_URL}>USGA: Handicap Index calculation</a>).
    The other 12 still sit in your record and can come back into the best 8 as older scores drop off. Because only the best scores
    count, a handicap shows what you can shoot on a good day, not your average day.
  </p>

  <h3>Fewer than 20 differentials</h3>
  <p>
    You need at least 3 scores (54 holes) to get an index. Until you have 20, fewer differentials count, and with very few rounds a
    small adjustment is taken off so a new index isn't set too high (<a href={ENGLAND_GOLF_INDEX_URL}>England Golf</a>):
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Scores in your record</th><th>Differentials used</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {FEW_ROUNDS.map(([rounds, rule]) => <tr><td>{rounds}</td><td>{rule}</td></tr>)}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Here is a record of five rounds:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Round</th><th>Adjusted score</th><th>Rating / slope</th><th>Differential</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {SAMPLE_DIFFERENTIALS.map(([round, score, course, differential]) => (
        <tr><td>{round}</td><td>{score}</td><td>{course}</td><td>{differential}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    With 5 scores the table says: use the lowest 1, with no adjustment. The lowest differential is 16.7, so the Handicap Index is
    <strong>16.7</strong>. Add a sixth round with a differential of 18.1 and the rule becomes the average of the lowest 2, minus 1.0:
    (16.7 + 18.1) ÷ 2 − 1.0 = <strong>16.4</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>An index can't go above <strong>54.0</strong> (<a href={RANDA_RULE_5_URL}>R&amp;A Rules of Handicapping, Rule 5</a>). A result below zero is a plus handicap, written like +1.5.</p>

  <h2>Caps and exceptional scores</h2>
  <p>
    Once you have an index, the WHS limits how fast it can rise, measured against your Low Handicap Index, the lowest index you had
    over the past year (<a href={RANDA_RULE_5_URL}>R&amp;A Rule 5.8</a>):
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Soft cap:</strong> any rise of more than 3.0 above your Low Handicap Index is halved.</li>
    <li><strong>Hard cap:</strong> your index can never be more than 5.0 above your Low Handicap Index.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    So if your Low Handicap Index is 12.0 and a run of bad rounds would put you at 16.0, the soft cap halves the 1.0 above 15.0 and
    you sit at 15.5. However bad things get, the hard cap stops you at 17.0.
  </p>
  <p>
    It works the other way too. An <strong>exceptional score</strong>, a differential 10.0 or more below your current index, cuts
    your index by 2.0 straight away (<a href={RANDA_RULE_5_URL}>R&amp;A Rule 5.9</a>).
  </p>

  <h2>From Handicap Index to Course Handicap</h2>
  <p>On the first tee you turn your index into strokes for that course (<a href={USGA_COURSE_HANDICAP_URL}>USGA: Course Handicap</a>):</p>
  <p><strong>Course Handicap = Handicap Index × (Slope Rating ÷ 113) + (Course Rating − Par)</strong></p>
  <p>
    Example: an index of 15.0 on tees rated 71.5 with a slope of 125, on a par 72. 15.0 × 125 ÷ 113 = 16.6, plus (71.5 − 72) = −0.5,
    gives 16.1, so you play off <strong>16</strong>. You receive one stroke on each of the 16 holes with the lowest stroke index on the
    card, and those strokes set your net double bogey on each hole.
  </p>

  <h2>How nine-hole scores are handled</h2>
  <p>
    Since the 2024 revision, a nine-hole score is combined with an expected score for the nine holes you didn't play, based on your
    Handicap Index, to make one 18-hole differential. It applies once you have 54 holes on record. The change took effect in the
    US on 16 January 2024 (<a href={USGA_2024_CHANGES_URL}>USGA: 2024 key changes</a>,
    <a href={GOLF_DIGEST_2024_URL}>Golf Digest</a>, <a href={USGA_EXPECTED_SCORE_URL}>USGA: expected score explained</a>).
  </p>

  <h2>Why an estimate can differ from an official index</h2>
  <p>
    An official Handicap Index comes from your club or national association, which has every score you post and the day's PCC. A
    calculator or an app works only with the rounds you give it, so its number is an estimate. Common reasons the two differ:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the estimate assumes a PCC of 0.0;</li>
    <li>it doesn't apply the soft cap, hard cap or exceptional score reduction, because it doesn't know your Low Handicap Index;</li>
    <li>scores you played elsewhere, or posted later, are missing;</li>
    <li>hole scores are capped differently.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Golf GPS Pro shows an <strong>estimated</strong> handicap index and its trend from the rounds you play with the app. It uses your
    tee's rating and slope, the differential formula and the few-rounds table above, and the best 8 of your last 20. It caps every
    hole at par + 5 rather than net double bogey, and for a nine-hole round it doubles that round's differential against half the
    course rating. It is not an official Handicap Index. For an official index, join a golf club or your national association.
    See <a href={sitePath('/golf-gps/handicap/')}>how the Golf GPS handicap tracker works</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions golfers ask</h2>
  <h3>Is my handicap my average score over par?</h3>
  <p>No. It is built from your best differentials, adjusted for each course's rating and slope, not an average of all your rounds.</p>
  <h3>How many rounds do I need for a handicap?</h3>
  <p>Three 18-hole scores, or the equivalent in nine-hole scores. The index then firms up as you approach 20.</p>
  <h3>Why did my handicap go up after one bad round?</h3>
  <p>Usually it didn't: a bad round rarely makes your best 8. What moves it is a good old score dropping out of your last 20.</p>

  <h2>Try the calculator</h2>
  <p>
    Enter three to twenty 18-hole rounds in the <a href={sitePath('/golf-handicap/calculator/')}>golf handicap calculator</a> and it
    applies the differential formula and the table above for you.
  </p>
</ArticlePage>
